WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.040
Why does a week of closure rewire a kitten's cortex, while months do nothing visible

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.040
in an adult?

3
00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:11.963
Every result so far depends on age.

4
00:00:12.883 --> 00:00:13.883
Two terms help.

5
00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:19.477
A sensitive period is a window in which experience has an unusually strong effect.

6
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:25.670
A critical period, in Eric Knudsen's definition, is a sensitive period with two

7
00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:26.670
extra features.

8
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.630
The experience is essential for normal development.

9
00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.430
And its effects last.

10
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.890
Real windows often fall between the two.

11
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.650
How sharp one looks depends on the species, the manipulation and the measure.

12
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:45.003
An adult cat's histogram may not move after a closure.

13
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:48.803
That does not mean the adult cortex cannot change at all.

14
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:53.233
Name the species with every number, because the window differs.

15
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:58.157
In kittens, susceptibility begins around the start of the fourth week.

16
00:00:58.977 --> 00:01:03.017
It stays high to about six to eight weeks, then declines through the third month.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.620
In macaques, early closure has its strongest effect in the first weeks after birth.

18
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.780
In mice, Joshua Gordon and Michael Stryker found the strongest shifts near postnatal

19
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.780
day 28.

20
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:19.763
The same few days of closure in adult mice did little.

21
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:23.073
Mice have no ocular dominance columns.

22
00:01:24.093 --> 00:01:27.993
Their value is genetics, used to ask what opens and closes the window.

23
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:35.507
Now the clinic. Amblyopia is reduced vision, usually in one eye, that glasses do not

24
00:01:35.507 --> 00:01:36.507
immediately restore.

25
00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.127
No structural disease of the eye explains it.

26
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:45.377
It usually arises when the two eyes give the young brain unequal input.

27
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.680
Common causes are strabismus, a large difference in focus between the eyes, and

28
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.540
anything that blocks one eye's image.

29
00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:57.163
Strabismus affects a few percent of children.

30
00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:03.447
Predict. A young child's left eye sees poorly after early strabismus.

31
00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:05.507
Glasses correct the optics.

32
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:07.467
Which eye do you patch?

33
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:19.130
The good eye. A patch covers it for part of the day, or atropine drops blur it.

34
00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:22.873
Then the weak eye carries the useful visual input.

35
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:31.763
For those hours the weak eye is the one helping the shared cells fire, so the

36
00:02:31.763 --> 00:02:33.083
contest tilts its way.

37
00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:38.323
That can work because the weak eye keeps pathways that can still be strengthened,

38
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:40.463
even if some connections were lost.

39
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:45.887
Treatment generally works best in young children, consistent with a sensitive

40
00:02:45.887 --> 00:02:49.687
period. Trials have found some older children still improve.

41
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.910
A cataract that blocks one eye from birth is usually removed early in infancy, for

42
00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:56.910
the same reason.

43
00:02:57.583 --> 00:03:00.363
Takeaway: the cell keeps the eye that helps it fire.

44
00:03:01.223 --> 00:03:03.903
That contest counts most while the window is open.

45
00:03:04.916 --> 00:03:08.736
What opens and closes that window is the next lesson's question.
